  "description": "This image may be one of the earliest surviving examples of a Cosmic Buddha, because he wears a crown and jewels, unlike the earthly Buddhas who are unadorned like monks. The Cosmic Buddhas are important in texts called tantras that explain methods of reaching enlightenment more quickly. The distinctive pointed shawl worn over his shoulders is a garment popular around 700 CE in territories ruled by the Shahi dynasty (c. 660s–822 CE), which extended from Afghanistan to Kashmir. This important sculpture has been rubbed over many centuries of worship involving ritually bathing the image.",
